  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 128 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nToday and Tonight:\n\nWater vapor imagery this afternoon continues to feature broad \ntroughing centered over the central third of the CONUS, with a \nshortwave embedded at the base sweeping shower and storm activity \nfarther to the east into Missouri and Iowa. Drilling down to \nthe surface, we find ourselves in a post-frontal airmass with \nthe front itself draped across the Southern Plains into the mid-\nMississippi Valley/Great Lakes region. Elevated shower and \nstorm activity is slated to continue across far southeast \nNebraska into Iowa for the next few hours before being further \nshunted off to the east, with easterly winds taking over at 5-10\nmph. Temperatures in the mid 70s to just over 80 degrees are \nset to cap off an excellent afternoon with increasingly clear \nskies -- setting the stage for fog chances overnight.\n\nWinds are set to decrease underneath the building nocturnal \ninversion overnight, as a ridge of high surface pressure helps calm \nconditions across the area. HREF model output signals very little \ninsulating cloud cover aside from some low stratus across far \nsoutheast Nebraska and Iowa, fog development likely where healthy \nrainfall occurred this morning. Crossover temperatures are only\n3-4 degrees above the overnight low, giving some uncertainty to\nthe density and southern extent of the fog. Some models do \nlimit its formation into Nebraska, favoring southern Iowa and \npoints northeast, from 4 AM to 9 AM tomorrow morning. \n\nThursday and Beyond:\n\nOnce winds begin to pick up Thursday morning and become more steady \nout of the southeast, lingering areas of fog should clear up and \nleave us with a slightly warmer, but still pleasant day that tops \nout in the 80s. Shower and storm chances should remain across \nnortheast Kansas and to the northwest along a surface trough draped \nacross north-central Nebraska into South Dakota. \n\nFriday into the weekend, we'll be plowing into a warming trend \nspurred on by mid-level height rises, a retreating jet stream, and \nbuilding thermal ridging just to the west of the area. One hangup \nwith the weekend forecast is just how much we will warm. The mix-\nhappy GFS/GEFS will steer the forecast towards highs that reach\nwell into the 100s while the rest of the global ensemble \nmembers have a much more tentative lean that sets highs in the \nupper 80s to lower 90s (which is where our forecast sits as \nwell). Surface data from the global models have a lingering \nboundary/front that sits across or near the forecast area, and \nserve as a focus for cloud/precipitation development that could \naffect the highs. A modest overlapping of ingredients for severe\nstorms stretches each day from Friday through much of next \nweek, peaking Saturday. Confidence isn't there quite yet to pin \nany locations down on a map, but don't be surprised if something\nshows up for tomorrow's issuance of the Day 3 Severe Outlook \nfrom the SPC.",
